<td data-sort-value="−1" id="mwCkM"><span typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:File" about="#mwt1122" id="mwCkQ" data-mw='{"caption":"Steady","parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Same","href":"./Template:Same"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><span title="Steady"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Steady2.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Steady2.svg/20px-Steady2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Steady2.svg/40px-Steady2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Steady" data-file-width="300" data-file-height="300" data-file-type="drawing" height="11" width="11" class="mw-file-element"/></span></span></td></tr>
<tr id="mwCkU">
<th id="mwCkY"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#Missouri" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwCkc">Missouri</a></th>
<td data-sort-value="3" style="background: #FFE3E3; color: black; vertical-align: middle; text-align: center; " class="table-no2 skin-invert" typeof="mw:Transclusion" about="#mwt1123" id="mwCkg" data-mw='{"parts":["|data-sort-value=3 ",{"template":{"target":{"wt":"no2","href":"./Template:No2"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"[[2025 Missouri redistricting|Districts left in place]]"}},"i":0}}]}'><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Missouri_redistricting" title="2025 Missouri redistricting" class="mw-redirect">Districts left in place</a></td>
<td id="mwCkk">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missouri_Legislature" title="Missouri Legislature" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCko">Missouri Legislature</a> passed maps to create a new safely Republican seat in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missouri's_5th_congressional_district" title="Missouri's 5th congressional district" id="mwCks">5th district</a>. Governor <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Kehoe" title="Mike Kehoe" id="mwCkw">Mike Kehoe</a> signed the map into law in September 2025.<sup about="#mwt1124" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:1_118-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":1"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:1-118" id="mwCk0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCk4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCk8">[</span>108<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClA">]</span></span></a></sup> The maps may be blocked by a referendum if enough signatures are gathered.<sup about="#mwt1127" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-149" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-149"}}'><a href="#cite_note-149" id="mwClE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClM">[</span>138<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClQ">]</span></span></a></sup> In March 2026, opponents of the map said they had enough signatures for the referendum to qualify.<sup about="#mwt1130" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Signatures_150-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Signatures"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Signatures-150"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Signatures-150" id="mwClU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClc">[</span>139<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClg">]</span></span></a></sup> On August 4, 2026, Missouri Secretary of State <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denny_Hoskins" title="Denny Hoskins" id="mwClk">Denny Hoskins</a> blocked the referendum from taking place, arguing that the referendum would be unconstitutional under <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constitution_of_Missouri" title="Constitution of Missouri" id="mwClo">Missouri's Constitution</a>,<sup about="#mwt1133" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-151" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-151"}}'><a href="#cite_note-151" id="mwCls"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl0">[</span>140<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl4">]</span></span></a></sup> a decision which was upheld by <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cole_County,_Missouri" title="Cole County, Missouri" id="mwCl8">Cole County</a> Circuit Judge Daniel Green on August 19.<sup about="#mwt1136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-152" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-152"}}'><a href="#cite_note-152" id="mwCmA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmI">[</span>141<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmM">]</span></span></a></sup> However, on September 3, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missouri_Supreme_Court" title="Missouri Supreme Court" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCmQ">Missouri Supreme Court</a> unanimously ruled that the referendum is sufficient and must be put on the ballot, thus leaving the 2022 maps in place for the 2026 elections.<sup about="#mwt1137" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-:6_119-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":":6"}}'><a href="#cite_note-:6-119" id="mwCmU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmc">[</span>109<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmg">]</span></span></a></sup> On September 8, U.S. Supreme Court Justice <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brett_Kavanaugh" title="Brett Kavanaugh" id="mwCmk">Brett Kavanaugh</a> denied an emergency appeal by State Republicans.<sup about="#mwt1140" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-153" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-153"}}'><a href="#cite_note-153" id="mwCmo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCms"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmw">[</span>142<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCm0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1143" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-154" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-154"}}'><a href="#cite_note-154" id="mwCm4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCm8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnA">[</span>143<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnE">]</span></span></a></sup> However, the same day, Chief U.S. District Judge <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_R._Clark" title="Stephen R. Clark" id="mwCnI">Stephen Clark</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_District_Court_for_the_Eastern_District_of_Missouri" title="United States District Court for the Eastern District of Missouri" id="mwCnM">Eastern District of Missouri</a> issued a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporary_restraining_order" title="Temporary restraining order" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCnQ">temporary restraining order</a> in a separate lawsuit prohibiting the state from using anything but the new 2025 maps.<sup about="#mwt1146" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-155" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-155"}}'><a href="#cite_note-155" id="mwCnU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCnY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnc">[</span>144<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCng">]</span></span></a></sup> On September 10, the U.S. Supreme Court granted an emergency stay of Judge Clark's order, putting the 2022 map back into place for the 2026 elections.<sup about="#mwt1149" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-156" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-156"}}'><a href="#cite_note-156" id="mwCnk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCno"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCns">[</span>145<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnw">]</span></span></a></sup> However, on September 21, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Court_of_Appeals_for_the_Eighth_Circuit" title="United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit" id="mwCn0">United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit</a>, ruling on the merits, affirmed the temporary restraining order and remanded the case to the lower court for entry of a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permanent_injunction" title="Permanent injunction" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCn4">permanent injunction</a> enjoining Missouri to use the 2025 map, but stayed their ruling for a week pending appeal to the U.S. Supreme Court.<sup about="#mwt1152" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-157" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-157"}}'><a href="#cite_note-157" id="mwCn8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCoA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoE">[</span>146<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoI">]</span></span></a></sup> On September 25, the U.S. Supreme Court unanimously held that state law rules in the case and granted an emergency stay of the Eighth Circuit's order and Judge Clark's injunction, leaving the 2022 maps in place for the 2026 elections.<sup about="#mwt1155" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-158" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-158"}}'><a href="#cite_note-158" id="mwCoM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCoQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoU">[</span>147<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoY">]</span></span></a></sup></td>
<td data-sort-value="1" id="mwCoc"><span typeof="mw:Transclusion mw:File" about="#mwt1156" id="mwCog" data-mw='{"caption":"Steady","parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Same","href":"./Template:Same"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}'><span title="Steady"><img resource="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Steady2.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Steady2.svg/20px-Steady2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Steady2.svg/40px-Steady2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="Steady" data-file-width="300" data-file-height="300" data-file-type="drawing" height="11" width="11" class="mw-file-element"/></span></span></td>
